.TH FTS 3
.SH NAME
fts \- traverse a file hierarchy
.SH LIBRARY
.IR LIBAST (3)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft CR
#include <fts.h>

FTS*     fts_open(char* const* \fIpaths\fP, int \fIflags\fP,
                  int (*\fIcomparf\fP)(FTSENT* const*, FTSENT* const*));
FTSENT*  fts_read(FTS* \fIfts\fP);
FTSENT*  fts_children(FTS* \fIfts\fP, int \fIflags\fP);
int      fts_set(FTS* \fIfts\fP, FTSENT* \fIf\fP, int \fIflags\fP);
int      fts_close(FTS* \fIfts\fP);

int      fts_flags(void);
int      fts_local(FTSENT* \fIf\fP);
int      fts_notify(int (*\fInotifyf\fP)(FTS*, FTSENT*, void*), void* \fIcontext\fP);
.ft
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B fts
functions are provided for traversing
Unix
file hierarchies.
A simple overview is that the
.B fts_open
function returns a
\[lq]handle\[rq]
on a file hierarchy, which is then supplied to
the other
.B fts
functions.
The
.B fts_read
function returns a pointer to a structure describing one of the files
in the file hierarchy.
The
.B fts_children
function returns a pointer to a linked list of structures, each of
which describes one of the files contained in a directory in the
hierarchy.
In general, directories are visited two distinguishable times; in pre-order
(before any of their descendants are visited) and in post-order (after all
of their descendants have been visited).
Files are visited once.
It is possible to walk the hierarchy
\[lq]logically\[rq],
i.e., visiting the files that symbolic links point to,
or physically (visiting the symbolic links themselves),
order the walk of the hierarchy, and
prune and/or re-visit portions of the hierarchy.
.PP
Two structures are defined (and typedef'd) in the include file
.IR <fts.h> .
The first is
\f[CB]FTS\fP,
the structure that represents the file hierarchy itself.
The second is
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP,
the structure that represents a file in the file
hierarchy.
Normally, an
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure is returned for every file in the file
hierarchy.
In this manual page,
.I file
and
\f[CI]FTSENT\fP
.I structure
are generally interchangeable.
.PP
The
\f[CB]FTS\fP
structure is opaque to the application except for two fields:
.IR fts_handle ,
a
.B void*
the application may use to store per-hierarchy data, and
.IR fts_errno ,
which holds the
.B errno
value for the most recent stream-level error (0 if none).
.PP
The
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure contains at least the following fields, which are
described in greater detail below:
.nf
.ft CR

typedef struct Ftsent {
        char*           fts_accpath;    /* path relative to .           */
        char*           fts_name;       /* file name                    */
        char*           fts_path;       /* path relative to top dir     */
        FTSENT*         fts_cycle;      /* offender if cycle            */
        FTSENT*         fts_link;       /* next child                   */
        FTSENT*         fts_parent;     /* parent directory             */
        struct stat*    fts_statp;      /* stat info                    */
        void*           fts_pointer;    /* local pointer value          */
        long            fts_number;     /* local numeric value          */
        int             fts_errno;      /* errno for this entry         */
        unsigned short  fts_info;       /* info flags                   */
        FTS*            fts;            /* fts_open() handle            */
        size_t          fts_namelen;    /* strlen(fts_name)             */
        size_t          fts_pathlen;    /* strlen(fts_path)             */
        ssize_t         fts_level;      /* file tree depth, 0 at top    */
} FTSENT;
.ft
.fi
.PP
These fields are defined as follows:
.TP
.I fts_info
One of the following values describing the returned
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure and
the file it represents.
With the exception of directories visited in pre-order
(\f[CB]FTS_D\fP),
all of these
entries are terminal, that is, they will not be revisited, nor will any
of their descendants be visited.
The
\f[CB]FTS_D\fP
value is combined (inclusive-or) with other flag bits to form
the composite values listed below it.
.RS
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_D\fP
A directory being visited in pre-order.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_DP\fP
A directory being visited in post-order.
The contents of the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure are unchanged from when
the directory was visited in pre-order, except for the
.I fts_info
field and the
.I fts_statp
field, which is re-stat'ed to update link counts and times.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_DC\fP
A directory that causes a cycle in the tree.
(The
.I fts_cycle
field of the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure will be filled in as well.)
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_DNR\fP
A directory which cannot be read.
This is returned, in place of
\f[CB]FTS_D\fP,
when the directory could not be entered and read.
This is an error return, and the
.I fts_errno
field will be set to indicate what caused the error.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_DNX\fP
A directory which cannot be searched (accessed).
This is returned when
.IR opendir (3)
on the directory fails for lack of search permission.
This is an error return, and the
.I fts_errno
field will be set to indicate what caused the error.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_DOT\fP
A file named
\f[CB].\fP
or
\f[CB]..\fP
which was not specified as a path name to
.B fts_open
(see
\f[CB]FTS_SEEDOT\fP).
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_F\fP
A regular file (not a directory or symbolic link).
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NS\fP
A file for which no
.IR stat (2)
information was available.
The contents of the
.I fts_statp
field are undefined.
This is an error return, and the
.I fts_errno
field will be set to indicate what caused the error.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NSOK\fP
A file for which no
.IR stat (2)
information was requested (see
\f[CB]FTS_NOSTAT\fP).
The contents of the
.I fts_statp
field are undefined.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_SL\fP
A symbolic link.
Symbolic links are only returned in physical mode (see
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP)
or when they reference a non-existent file.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_SLNONE\fP
A symbolic link with a non-existent target.
The contents of the
.I fts_statp
field reference the file characteristic information for the symbolic link
itself.
.RE
.TP
.I fts_accpath
A path for accessing the file from the current directory.
Unless the
\f[CB]FTS_NOCHDIR\fP
option was specified, this is the same as
.I fts_name ,
valid only while the traversal is positioned in the file's parent
directory; otherwise it is the same as
.IR fts_path .
.TP
.I fts_path
The path for the file relative to the root of the traversal.
This path contains the path specified to
.B fts_open
as a prefix.
A leading
\f[CB]./\fP
is omitted unless the
\f[CB]FTS_SEEDOTDIR\fP
option is in effect.
.TP
.I fts_pathlen
The length of the string referenced by
.IR fts_path .
.TP
.I fts_name
The name of the file.
.TP
.I fts_namelen
The length of the string referenced by
.IR fts_name .
.TP
.I fts_level
The depth of the traversal, numbered from 0 to N, where this file
was found.
The
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure representing the parent of the starting point (or root)
of the traversal is numbered \-1, and the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure for the root
itself is numbered 0.
.TP
.I fts_errno
Upon return of a
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure from the
.B fts_children
or
.B fts_read
functions, with its
.I fts_info
field set to
\f[CB]FTS_DNR\fP,
\f[CB]FTS_DNX\fP,
\f[CB]FTS_NS\fP
or
\f[CB]FTS_ERR\fP,
the
.I fts_errno
field contains the value of the external variable
.B errno
specifying the cause of the error.
Otherwise, the contents of the
.I fts_errno
field are undefined.
.TP
.I fts_number
This field is provided for the use of the application program and is
not modified by the
.B fts
functions.
It is initialized to 0.
.TP
.I fts_pointer
This field is provided for the use of the application program and is
not modified by the
.B fts
functions.
It is initialized to
\f[CB]NULL\fP.
.TP
.I fts_parent
A pointer to the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure referencing the file in the hierarchy
immediately above the current file, i.e., the directory of which this
file is a member.
A parent structure for the initial entry point is provided as well;
only its
.IR fts_level ,
.I fts_number
and
.I fts_pointer
fields are guaranteed to be initialized.
.TP
.I fts_link
Upon return from the
.B fts_children
function, the
.I fts_link
field points to the next structure in the
\f[CB]NULL\fP-terminated
linked list of directory members.
Otherwise, the contents of the
.I fts_link
field are undefined.
.TP
.I fts_cycle
If a directory causes a cycle in the hierarchy (see
\f[CB]FTS_DC\fP),
either because
of a hard link between two directories, or a symbolic link pointing to a
directory, the
.I fts_cycle
field of the structure will point to the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure in the hierarchy that references the same file as the current
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure.
Otherwise, the contents of the
.I fts_cycle
field are undefined.
.TP
.I fts_statp
A pointer to
.IR stat (2)
information for the file.
.TP
.I fts
A pointer to the
\f[CB]FTS\fP
stream handle returned by
.BR fts_open .
.PP
A single buffer is used for all of the paths of all of the files in the
file hierarchy.
Therefore, the
.I fts_path
and
.I fts_accpath
fields are guaranteed to be NUL-terminated
.I only
for the file most recently returned by
.BR fts_read .
To use these fields to reference any files represented by other
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures will require that the path buffer be modified using the
information contained in that
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure's
.I fts_pathlen
field.
Any such modifications should be undone before further calls to
.B fts_read
are attempted.
The
.I fts_name
field is always NUL-terminated.
.SS fts_open
The
.B fts_open
function takes a pointer to an array of character pointers naming one
or more paths which make up a logical file hierarchy to be traversed.
The array must be terminated by a
\f[CB]NULL\fP
pointer.
If the
\f[CB]FTS_ONEPATH\fP
option is set,
.I paths
is instead a single
.BR "const char *"
naming the lone path to traverse.
If
.I paths
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP,
or (without
\f[CB]FTS_ONEPATH\fP)
the array is empty, the path
\f[CB].\fP
is traversed.
.PP
There are
a number of options, at least one of which (either
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
or
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP)
governs the treatment of symbolic links.
The options are selected by
.IR or 'ing
the following values:
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
This option (value 0, the default) causes the
.B fts
routines to return
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures for the targets of symbolic links
instead of the symbolic links themselves.
If this option is in effect, the only symbolic links for which
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures
are returned to the application are those referencing non-existent files.
Exactly one of
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
or
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP
is in effect for any traversal;
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
is the default when
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP
is not specified.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP
This option causes the
.B fts
routines to return
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures for symbolic links themselves instead
of the target files they point to.
If this option is set,
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures for all symbolic links in the
hierarchy are returned to the application.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_META\fP
This option causes any symbolic link specified as a root path to be
followed immediately, whether or not
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
is also in effect.
It is also available under the name
\f[CB]FTS_COMFOLLOW\fP.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOCHDIR\fP
To allow descending to arbitrary depths
(independent of
\f[CB]PATH_MAX\fP)
and improve performance, the
.B fts
functions change directories as they walk the file hierarchy.
This has the side-effect that an application cannot rely on being
in any particular directory during the traversal.
The
\f[CB]FTS_NOCHDIR\fP
option turns off this feature, and the
.B fts
functions will not change the current directory.
Note that applications should not themselves change their current directory
and try to access files unless
\f[CB]FTS_NOCHDIR\fP
is specified and absolute
pathnames were provided as arguments to
.BR fts_open .
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOSTAT\fP
By default, returned
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures reference file characteristic information (the
.I fts_statp
field) for each file visited.
This option relaxes that requirement as a performance optimization,
allowing the
.B fts
functions to set the
.I fts_info
field to
\f[CB]FTS_NSOK\fP
and leave the contents of the
.I fts_statp
field undefined.
The roots and any directories encountered during traversal are still
fully populated.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_SEEDOT\fP
By default, unless they are specified as path arguments to
.BR fts_open ,
any files named
\f[CB].\fP
or
\f[CB]..\fP
encountered in the file hierarchy are ignored.
This option causes the
.B fts
routines to return
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures for them (as
\f[CB]FTS_DOT\fP).
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_SEEDOTDIR\fP
Retain a leading
\f[CB]./\fP
in returned
.I fts_path
values.
This is the default when a root path itself begins with
\f[CB].\fP
when
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL\fP
is in effect.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOSEEDOTDIR\fP
Never retain a leading
\f[CB]./\fP
in returned
.I fts_path
values; such prefixes are stripped from root paths.
This option overrides
\f[CB]FTS_SEEDOTDIR\fP.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_TOP\fP
Do not traverse subdirectories; the root paths are returned as terminal
entries.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOPREORDER\fP
Do not return pre-order
(\f[CB]FTS_D\fP)
visits for directories.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOPOSTORDER\fP
Do not return post-order
(\f[CB]FTS_DP\fP)
visits for directories.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_XDEV\fP
This option prevents
.B fts
from descending into directories that have a different device number
than the file from which the descent began.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_ONEPATH\fP
The
.I paths
argument is a single
.BR "const char *"
rather than a
\f[CB]NULL\fP-terminated array of paths.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_USER\fP
The first option bit available for application use; the
.B fts
functions ignore it.
.PP
The
.I comparf
argument points to a user-defined function which may be used to order
the traversal of the hierarchy.
It
takes two pointers to pointers to
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures as arguments and
should return a negative value, zero, or a positive value to indicate
if the file referenced by its first argument comes before, in any order
with respect to, or after, the file referenced by its second argument.
The
.IR fts_accpath ,
.I fts_path
and
.I fts_pathlen
fields of the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures may
.I never
be used in this comparison.
If the
.I fts_info
field is set to
\f[CB]FTS_NS\fP
or
\f[CB]FTS_NSOK\fP,
the
.I fts_statp
field may not either.
If the
.I comparf
argument is
\f[CB]NULL\fP,
the directory traversal order is in the order listed in
.I paths
for the root paths, and in the order listed in the directory for
everything else.
.SS fts_read
The
.B fts_read
function returns a pointer to an
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure describing a file in
the hierarchy.
Directories (that are readable and do not cause cycles) are visited at
least twice, once in pre-order and once in post-order.
All other files are visited at least once.
(Hard links between directories that do not cause cycles or symbolic
links to symbolic links may cause files to be visited more than once,
or directories more than twice.)
.PP
If all the members of the hierarchy have been returned,
.B fts_read
returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP.
If an error unrelated to a file in the hierarchy occurs,
.B fts_read
returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP
and sets the
.I fts_errno
field of the stream handle appropriately.
If an error related to a returned file occurs, a pointer to an
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure is returned (see
.IR fts_info ).
.PP
The
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures returned by
.B fts_read
may be overwritten after a call to
.B fts_close
on the same file hierarchy stream, or, after a call to
.B fts_read
on the same file hierarchy stream unless they represent a directory,
in which case they will not be overwritten until the directory's
post-order
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure has been returned.
.SS fts_children
The
.B fts_children
function returns a pointer to an
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure describing the first entry in a
\f[CB]NULL\fP-terminated
linked list of
the files in the directory represented by the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure most recently returned by
.BR fts_read .
The list is linked through the
.I fts_link
field of the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure, and is ordered by the user-specified comparison function, if any.
Repeated calls to
.B fts_children
will recreate this linked list.
.PP
As a special case, if
.B fts_read
has not yet been called for a hierarchy,
.B fts_children
will return a pointer to the files in the logical directory specified to
.BR fts_open ,
i.e., the arguments specified to
.BR fts_open .
Otherwise, if the
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure most recently returned by
.B fts_read
is not a directory being visited in pre-order,
or the directory does not contain any files,
.B fts_children
returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP.
If an error occurs,
.B fts_children
returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP
and sets the stream
.I fts_errno
appropriately.
.PP
The
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structures returned by
.B fts_children
may be overwritten after a call to
.BR fts_children ,
.B fts_close
or
.B fts_read
on the same file hierarchy stream.
.PP
.I flags
may be set to
\f[CB]FTS_NOSTAT\fP
to indicate that the file characteristic information in the returned
list is not needed;
the contents of the
.I fts_statp
fields are then undefined.
.SS fts_set
The
.B fts_set
function allows the user application to determine further processing
for the file
.I f ,
which must be the entry most recently returned by
.BR fts_read .
If the
.I fts
argument is
\f[CB]NULL\fP,
it defaults to the current
\f[CB]FTS\fP
handle stored in the provided
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure.
It is an error for the provided
\f[CB]FTSENT\fP
structure not to refer to the current file.
The
.B fts_set
function
returns 0 on success, and \-1 if an error occurs.
Its
.I flags
argument must have one of the following values:
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_AGAIN\fP
Re-visit the file; any file type may be re-visited.
The next call to
.B fts_read
will return the referenced file.
The
.I fts_statp
and
.I fts_info
fields of the structure will be reinitialized at that time,
but no other fields will have been changed.
This option is meaningful only for the most recently returned
file from
.BR fts_read .
Normal use is for post-order directory visits, where it causes the
directory to be re-visited (in both pre- and post-order) as well as all
of its descendants.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_FOLLOW\fP
The referenced file must be a symbolic link.
The next call to
.B fts_read
returns the file with the
.I fts_info
and
.I fts_statp
fields reinitialized to reflect the target of the symbolic link instead
of the symbolic link itself.
If the target of the symbolic link does not exist, the fields of the
returned structure are unchanged and the
.I fts_info
field is set to
\f[CB]FTS_SLNONE\fP.
If the target of the link is a directory, the pre-order return, followed
by the return of all of its descendants, followed by a post-order return,
is done.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_SKIP\fP
No descendants of this file are visited.
The file must be a directory being visited in pre-order
(\f[CB]FTS_D\fP).
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_NOPOSTORDER\fP
Suppress the post-order
(\f[CB]FTS_DP\fP)
visit for this directory.
.TP
\f[CB]FTS_STAT\fP
Inform
.B fts
that the application has already obtained
.IR stat (2)
information for the file in
.IR f->fts_statp ;
it will not be re-stat'ed.
.SS fts_close
The
.B fts_close
function closes a file hierarchy stream
.I fts
and, unless the
\f[CB]FTS_NOCHDIR\fP
option was specified, restores the current directory to the directory
from which
.B fts_open
was called to open
.IR fts .
It returns 0.
.SS fts_flags
The
.B fts_flags
function returns the default traversal flags implied by the
.IR astconf (3)
\[lq]PATH_RESOLVE\fP
configuration:
\f[CB]FTS_LOGICAL\fP
if
\(lqlogical\(rq,
\f[CB]FTS_PHYSICAL|FTS_SEEDOTDIR\fP
if
\(lqphysical\(rq,
and
\f[CB]FTS_META|FTS_PHYSICAL|FTS_SEEDOTDIR\fP
otherwise.
.SS fts_local
The
.B fts_local
function returns non-zero if the file described by
.I f
resides on a local file system, 0 otherwise.
.SS fts_notify
The
.B fts_notify
function registers or deregisters a notification function called as
.IB notifyf ( fts ", " f ", " context )
for each entry returned by
.BR fts_read .
If
.I context
is not
\f[CB]NULL\fP,
.I notifyf
is added to the notification list;
if
.I context
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP,
the first registered occurrence of
.I notifyf
is removed.
A return value greater than 0 from
.I notifyf
stops the traversal of the current entry;
a value less than 0 aborts the traversal with a stream error.
.B fts_notify
returns 0 on success and \-1 on error.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The
.B fts_open
function returns a pointer to the new
.B fts
stream on success and
\f[CB]NULL\fP
on failure.
.PP
The
.B fts_read
function returns a pointer to the next file entry on success, or if an
error occurs that relates specifically to that file entry.
On reaching the end of the file hierarchy, or on failure, it returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP;
in the latter case the stream
.I fts_errno
field is set to an appropriate non-zero value.
.PP
The
.B fts_children
function returns a pointer to a linked list of file entries on
success.
On reaching the end of the file hierarchy it returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP.
On failure, it returns
\f[CB]NULL\fP
and sets the stream
.I fts_errno
field to an appropriate non-zero value.
.PP
The
.B fts_set
function returns 0 on success and \-1 if its
.I flags
argument is invalid or
.I f
is not the current entry.
.PP
The
.B fts_close
function returns 0.
.SH ERRORS
The
.B fts_open
function may fail and set
.B errno
for any of the errors specified for the library functions
.IR open (2)
and
.IR malloc (3).
.PP
The
.B fts_close
function may fail and set
.B errno
for any of the errors specified for the library functions
.IR chdir (2)
and
.IR close (2).
.PP
The
.B fts_read
and
.B fts_children
functions may fail and set
.B errno
for any of the errors specified for the library functions
.IR chdir (2),
.IR malloc (3),
.IR opendir (3),
.IR readdir (3)
and
.IR stat (2).
.PP
In addition, the
.BR fts_children ,
.B fts_open
and
.B fts_set
functions may fail and set
.B errno
as follows:
.TP
\f[CB]EINVAL\fP
The options were invalid, or the list was empty.
.SH CAVEATS
To avoid conflicts with native OS
.IR fts (3)
implementations, this
.IR LIBAST (3)
version defines all the function names as macros to prefix
.RB \(lq _ast_ \(rq
to their actual names. If you
.B #undef
them, you get what you paid for.
